# Good-Guy-Economy model configuration — the ONE file to edit for a model swap or a
# hardware/machine move.
#
# After editing, apply with: good-guy-economy-apply-model

# Which flags good-guy-economy-llm-run passes to the server binary:
#   vulkan (default) — GPU offload via llama.cpp's Vulkan backend. Uses
#                       GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_GPU_DEVICE and pins VK_DRIVER_FILES.
#   cpu               — a CPU-only build/fork (e.g. a machine with no
#                       supported GPU backend). No -dev/-ngl/VK_DRIVER_FILES;
#                       uses GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_THREADS instead.
GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_BACKEND=vulkan

# llama.cpp backend build to run. For GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_BACKEND=vulkan this is
# llama.cpp's Vulkan build (works across GPU vendors; no CUDA/ROCm required).
# For GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_BACKEND=cpu, point this at your CPU-only build/fork instead.
GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_LLAMA_SERVER_BIN=$HOME/.lmstudio/extensions/backends/llama.cpp-linux-x86_64-vulkan-avx2-2.27.1/llama-server
GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_LLAMA_LIB_DIR=$HOME/.lmstudio/extensions/backends/llama.cpp-linux-x86_64-vulkan-avx2-2.27.1

# The model file to load. Swap this line to change models (e.g. to
# Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-Q4_K_XL once downloaded, or any future GGUF).
GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_MODEL_PATH=$HOME/models/unsloth/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-MTP-GGUF/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-Q4_K_M.gguf

# --- GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_BACKEND=vulkan only ---
#
# Vulkan device(s) to offload to (see `llama-server --list-devices`). As of
# 2026-08-20 the iGPU no longer enumerates under Vulkan on this machine, so
# Vulkan0 and Vulkan1 are the two discrete WX9100s (re-verified after
# good-guy-economy-llm.service failed to start with the old Vulkan1,Vulkan2 mapping —
# `llama-server --list-devices` showed only 2 devices, both WX9100s).
# Comma-separated list = multi-GPU. Re-check this on a different
# machine/hardware change.
# 2026-08-23: good-guy-economy-llm-run now exports VK_DRIVER_FILES pointing at the
# AMD-only Vulkan ICD, so the Intel iGPU does not enumerate at all. That
# matters because the iGPU allocates SYSTEM RAM as VRAM -- when it briefly
# held Vulkan0, starting this service OOM-killed the CPU dispatcher.
# With only the AMD driver loaded, Vulkan0 and Vulkan1 ARE the two WX9100s
# and the indices can no longer drift. Verify: llama-server --list-devices
GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_GPU_DEVICE=Vulkan0,Vulkan1

# --- GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_BACKEND=cpu only ---
#
# CPU threads for generation. Also used (as -t) when BACKEND=vulkan, since
# some ops still run on CPU either way; on this machine that's physical-core
# count (6). Set to your target machine's physical core count.
#GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_THREADS=6

# --- either backend ---
#
# KV cache precision and flash attention. Defaults below match what this
# machine runs; override only if your backend/fork needs something else
# (e.g. a CPU fork that doesn't support flash attention: GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_FLASH_ATTN=off).
#GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_CACHE_TYPE_K=q8_0
#GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_CACHE_TYPE_V=q8_0
#GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_FLASH_ATTN=on

# Escape hatch: extra raw flags appended verbatim to the launch command, for
# anything a particular backend/fork needs that isn't covered above.
#GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_EXTRA_ARGS=

# Self-speculative decoding off the model's own built-in MTP head (no -md
# draft file). Qwen3.6-35B-A3B has one, so this defaults "on" to match the
# original deployment. Most other models -- including LFM2.5 (lfm2moe arch,
# no MTP head) -- do NOT have this and will error or behave oddly with the
# flag forced on. Set to "off" for any model without a native MTP head.
#GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_SPEC_DECODE=on

# Context window size (tokens), shared across both -np 2 -kvu slots
# dynamically (see good-guy-economy-investigator memory). Model's true native max is
# 262144 (Qwen3.6-35B-A3B's trained context — llama.cpp silently clamps to
# this and warns "possible training context overflow" if you request more,
# confirmed 2026-08-10 by testing up to 524288). This is backed off from
# that ceiling for margin, not VRAM-limited — VRAM headroom at the full
# 262144 native max is still ~9.4% (28.98GB/31.97GB) with -np 2 -kvu.
# Must match `hermes config set model.context_length` — good-guy-economy-apply-model
# keeps these in sync.
# Context. MEASURED 2026-08-23 with Gemma co-resident on the CPU.
# VRAM is full at ~29 GB/32 GB, so KV beyond that spills into GTT -- pinned
# SYSTEM RAM -- and competes directly with Gemma. Measured MemAvailable:
#   229376 -> GTT 5687 MB -> only   17 MB free  (this is what OOM'd the box)
#   131072 -> GTT 2149 MB ->      3103 MB free
#    65536 -> GTT 1123 MB ->      4150 MB free
# 131072 is the safe default: still a 131K window, with 3 GB of headroom.
# Raising this back toward 229376 is only safe with Gemma stopped.
GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_CTX=131072

# Concurrent request slots on port 8099. Was hardcoded at 2, raised 2026-08-23.
# Three clients share this server -- Good-Guy-Economy investigating, opencode, and the
# Gemma dispatcher asking general questions -- so at 2 slots the third caller
# silently QUEUES, which reads as a hang to a non-technical user.
# -kvu means the KV cache is one shared pool of GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_CTX rather than
# GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_CTX/slots each, so extra slots cost compute buffers, not KV.
GOOD_GUY_ECONOMY_SLOTS=4
